Let $V = \bigoplus_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} V_{(n)}$ be a simple vertex operator algebra such that:
(i) $V_{(0)} = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}$ and $V_{(n)} = 0$ for $n < 0$;
(ii) the contragredient module $V'$ is isomorphic to $V$ as a $V$-module;
(iii) $V$ is $C_2$-cofinite, i.e. $\dim V/C_2(V) < \infty$, where $C_2(V) = \mathrm{span}\{\mathrm{Res}_x x^{-2} Y(u,x)v : u,v \in V\}$;
(iv) every grading-restricted generalized $V$-module is completely reducible.

Let $G$ be a finite group of automorphisms of $V$. For each $g \in G$, consider the category of $g$-twisted (grading-restricted generalized) $V$-modules, and consider twisted intertwining operators of types $\binom{W_3}{W_1\, W_2}$ where $W_i$ is a $g_i$-twisted $V$-module ($g_i\in G$).

Question: Do these twisted intertwining operators (across all $g\in G$) satisfy the associativity property, the commutativity property, and the modular invariance property (the analogues, in the twisted/orbifold setting, of the corresponding properties known for untwisted intertwining operators among $V$-modules)?

